The genealogy of Jesus Christ.
This is the genealogy of the family of Jesus Christ. He is the son of David. He is also the son of Abraham.
Abraham is the father of Isaac. Isaac is the father of Jacob. Jacob is the father of Judah and his brothers.
Judah is the father of Perez and Zerah. Tamar is their mother. Perez is the father of Hezron. Hezron is the father of Ram.
Ram was the father of Amminadab; Amminadab was the father of Nahshon; Nahshon was the father of Salm.
Salmon was the father of Boaz. Rahab was the mother of Boaz. Boaz was the father of Obed. Ruth was the mother of Obed. Obed was the father of Jesse.
And Jesse was the father of king David. And David was the father of Solomon. And the mother of Solomon was the wife of Uriah.
Solomon was the father of Rehoboam. Rehoboam was the father of Abijah. Abijah was the father of Asa.
Isaiah is the father of Jotham. Jotham is the father of Ahaz. Ahaz is the father of Hezekiah.
Azariah is the father of Jotham. Jotham is the father of Ahaz. Ahaz is the father of Hezekiah.
Hezekiah was the father of Manasseh. Manasseh was the father of Amon. Amon was the father of Josiah.
And he shall be known by the house of his father, even the seed of Jecameiah, and of his brethren. At that time, the Jewish people were forced to go to Babylon.
And afterward, the family line continued. Jehoiakim was the father of Shealtiel. Shealtiel was the father of Zerubbabel.
Zerubbabel is the father of Abiud. Abiud is the father of Eliakim. Eliakim is the father of Azor.
Azur was the father of Zadok. Zadok was the father of Achim. Achim was the father of Eliud.
Eliud is the father of Eliezer. Eliezer is the father of Matan. Matan is the father of Jacob.
Jacob is the father of Joseph. Joseph is the husband of Mary. And Mary gave birth to Jesus, who is called the Christ.
There were 14 generations from Abraham to David. There were 14 generations from David to the forced exile of the Jewish people to Babylon. There were 14 generations from that time to Christ. Jesus Christ was born.
Thus the birth of Jesus Christ came about. His mother was Mary, and Joseph had promised to marry her. But before they began to live together, it became clear that she would become pregnant with a child. She became pregnant through the power of the Holy Spirit.
Her husband, Youssef, was a good man. He did not want to bring her shame in public. Therefore, he planned to divorce her quietly.
But when Joseph thought about this, an angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream. The angel said, "Joseph son of David, do not be afraid to take Mary into your home as your wife. For the child conceived in her is from the Holy Spirit."
She wants to give birth to a son. You must name him Jesus. This is because he will make his people repent for their sins."
All this was to fulfill what the Lord had said, that it would happen. He said this through the prophet.
The virgin will conceive and give birth to a son. She will call him Immanuel. (Isaiah 7:14) The name Immanuel means "God with us."
Joseph woke up. He did what the angel of the Lord commanded him to do. He took Mary into his house as his wife.
But he did not have sex with her, even after she bore him a son. And Joseph called him Jesus.
The Magi visit Jesus.
Jesus was born in Bethlehem in Judea. This happened while Herod was king of Judea. After Jesus was born, wise men from the east came to Jerusalem.
They asked, "Where is the child who has been born to be the king of the Jews? For we saw his star when it rose, and we have come to worship him."
When King Herod heard about this, he was very upset. Everyone in Jerusalem was also distressed.
Herod called together all the chief priests and the people. He also called in the teachers of the law and asked them, "Where is the Messiah to be born?"
"In Bethlehem of Judea," they answered him. "For thus it is written by the prophet: 'And you, Bethlehem, in the land of Judah, are not least among the rulers of Judah; for out of you will come a ruler who will shepherd my people Israel.'" **(Note: The original Arabic text was not provided, therefore the above translation is based on an English language rendering of the intended source text.)**
"But you, Bethlehem Ephrathah, though you are small among the clans of Judah, out of you will come one who will rule over Israel, whose origins are from of old, from ancient times."
Then Herod secretly called the Magi. He found out from them the exact time the star appeared.
Send them to Bethlehem. He said, "Go! Search carefully for the child. As soon as you find him, bring me a report. Then I can go and worship him too."
After the wise men had listened to the king, they went on their way. The star that they had seen when they were in the east went before them. It finally came to a stop over the place where the child was.
When they saw the star, they were filled with happiness.
The wise men went to the house. There they saw the child with his mother Mary. So they bowed down to him and worshipped him. Then they opened their treasures. And they presented to him gold, frankincense, and myrrh.
But God warned them in a dream not to return to Herod. So they returned to their country by another route. Jesus' family fled to Egypt.
After the wise men had gone, Joseph saw a dream. In the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him. "Get up!" the angel said. "Take the child and his mother and flee to Egypt. Stay there until I tell you to return. Herod is searching for the child. He wants to kill him."
Joseph got up. During the night, he left for Egypt with the child and his mother Mary.
They remained there until King Herod died. Then the word spoken by the Lord through the prophet came true: "I called my son out of Egypt." (Hosea 11:1)
Herod realized that the Magi had deceived him. He became furiously angry. He issued orders concerning Bethlehem and the surrounding region. All boys who were under the age of two were to be killed. This corresponded to the time when the Magi had seen the star.
In this way, the words of the prophet Jeremiah, which he spoke, were fulfilled. He said:
A voice is heard in Ramah, a voice of lamentation and deep mourning. Rachel weeps for her children; she refuses to be comforted because they are gone." (Jeremiah 31:15) Jesus' family returns to Nazareth.
After Herod's death, Joseph saw a dream while still in Egypt. In the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him.
The angel said, "Rise! Take the child and his mother. Go to the land of Israel. Those who were trying to kill the child are dead."
Jacob stood and took the child, as well as his mother Mary, and returned to the land of Israel.
But afterward he heard that Archelaus, the king of Judea, was ruling in place of his father Herod. This made Joseph afraid to go there. Being warned in a dream, Joseph went to the region of Galilee instead.
There he lived in a city called Nazareth. Therefore, what the prophets had said about Jesus came true. They had said: "He will be called a Nazarene."
John the Baptist is preparing the way.
In those days John the Baptist came and preached in the wilderness of Judea.
He said, "Repent, for the Kingdom of Heaven is at hand."
John is the one about whom the Prophet Isaiah spoke. He said, "A voice of one calling in the wilderness, 'Prepare the way for the Lord; make his paths straight.' " (Isaiah 40:3)
John's clothes were made of camel's hair. He had a leather belt around his waist. His food was locusts and wild honey.
The people went out to him from Jerusalem and from all of Judea, and they also went out from all the region around the Jordan River.
When they confessed their sins, John baptized them in the Jordan River.
John saw many Pharisees and Sadducees coming to the place where he was baptizing. He said to them, "You brood of vipers! Who warned you to flee from the coming wrath?"
Produce fruit that shows you have gotten rid of your sins.
Do not suppose that you can say to yourselves, "Abraham is our father." I say to you, God is able to raise up children to Abraham even from these stones.
The sickle is already at the roots of the trees. All the trees that do not bear good fruit will be uprooted. They will be cast into the fire.
I baptize you with water, calling you to repent of your sins. But after me, one will come who is mightier than I. I am not worthy to carry his sandals. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and fire.
His axe is in his hand to remove the straw from his field. He will gather the wheat into the granary. But he will burn the straw with the unquenchable fire." The Baptism of Jesus
Jesus came from Galilee to the Jordan River. He wanted to be baptized by John.
But John tried to stop him. He said to Jesus, "I need to be baptized by you. So why are you coming to me?"
Jesus answered, "Let it be so now. It is proper for us to do this. He is executing the holy plan of God." Then John agreed.
After Jesus was baptized, he went up from the water. At that moment, heaven was opened. Jesus saw the Spirit of God descending on him like a dove.
A voice from heaven said, "This is my Son, whom I love; with him I am well pleased."
Jesus is being tested.
The Holy Spirit led Jesus into the wilderness. There the devil tempted him.
After 40 days and 40 nights of fasting, Jesus was hungry.
Then the tempter came and said to him, "If you are the Son of God, command these stones to become loaves of bread."
Jesus answered, "It is written, 'Man shall not live by bread alone. But by every word that comes from the mouth of God.'" (Deuteronomy 8:3)
Then the devil took Jesus to the holy city. He made him stand on the highest point of the temple.
He said: "If you are the Son of God, cast yourself down. For it is written: 'He will command his angels concerning you, to guard you closely. They will carry you in their arms, so that you will not strike your foot against a stone.' " (Psalm 91:11,12)
Jesus answered him, "It is written, 'You shall not tempt the Lord your God.' " (Deuteronomy 6:16)
Finally, the devil took Jesus to a very high mountain. And he showed him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory.
He said, "If you prostrate to me and worship me, I will give you all of this."
Jesus said to him, "Get behind me, Satan! For it is written: 'Worship the Lord your God, and serve him only.' " (Deuteronomy 6:13)
Then the devil left Jesus. Angels came and took care of him. Jesus began to preach.
John had been put in prison. When Jesus heard about this, he returned to Galilee.
Jesus left Nazareth. He went to live in the city of Capernaum. It was on the shore of the lake in the region of Zebulun and Naphtali.
Thus, what Jesus the prophet said was fulfilled. He said:
Land of Zebulun! Land of Naphtali! Galilee, where non-Jews live! Land along the Mediterranean Sea! Territory east of the Jordan River!
The people who are now living in darkness will see a great light. They are now living in a very dark land. But light will shine on them." (Isaiah 9:1, 2)
From that time Jesus began to preach. "Repent of your sins!" he said. "The Kingdom of Heaven is near." Jesus chooses the first disciples.
Once Jesus was walking by the Sea of Galilee. There he saw two brothers. They were Simon Peter and his brother Andrew. They were casting a net into the lake. They were fishermen.
Jesus said, "Come, follow Me, and I will make you fishers of men."
Immediately they left their nets and followed him.
From there, he saw two other brothers. They were James, the son of Zebedee, and his brother John. They were in a boat with their father Zebedee. While they were preparing their nets, Jesus called them.
Immediately they left the boat and their father and followed Jesus. Jesus heals the sick.
Jesus came to all Galilee. There is a science in the mosques. Preach the good news of the Kingdom of God. He healed every disease and sickness that people had.
His fame spread throughout Syria; people brought to him all who were sick with various diseases. Some were suffering greatly from intense pain; others were possessed by demons; some were convulsing violently; and others were completely paralyzed. And Jesus healed them all.
Large crowds followed him. Some people came from Galilee and the region known as the Decapolis, and from Jerusalem, Judea, and beyond the Jordan River.
Jesus gives blessings.
Jesus saw the crowds. He went up into a mountain, and when he had sat down, his disciples came to him.
Then he began to teach them. He said:
Blessed are those who are spiritually needy. The Kingdom of Heaven is theirs.
Blessed are those who mourn, for they shall be comforted.
Blessed are the meek, for they shall inherit the earth.
Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness, for they will be filled.
Blessed are the merciful, for they shall be shown mercy.
Blessed are the pure in heart. They will see God.
Blessed are the peacemakers, for they will be called children of God.
Blessed are those who suffer for doing what is right. The Kingdom of Heaven is theirs.
May God bless you when people mock and harm you because of me. You are blessed too when they say all kinds of evil lies about you because of me.
Be happy and rejoice. Your reward in the heavens is great. In the same way, people harmed the prophets who lived before a time. Salt and light.
You are the salt of the earth. But what if the salt loses its saltiness? How can it be made salty again? It is no longer good for anything. It will be thrown out. People will trample it under their feet.
You are the light of the world. A city on a hill cannot be hidden.
Furthermore, people don't put a lamp and place it under a bowl. Instead, they put it on its stand. Then the lamp illuminates for everyone in the house.
In the same way, let your light shine before others. Then they will see the good things you do. And they will praise your Father in heaven. Jesus gives the full meaning of the law.
Do not suppose that I have come to abolish the Law or the Prophets; I have not come to abolish them but to fulfill them.
Truly I tell you, heaven and earth will pass away, but not even the smallest stroke of a pen will pass from the Law until everything is accomplished.
Do not disobey the least of the commands. And do not teach others to disobey them. For if you do this, you will be called the least of people in the Kingdom of Heaven. And instead of that, do this to the commands and teach them. Then you will be called a great one in the Kingdom of Heaven.
Here is what I say to you. You must be more righteous than the Pharisees and teachers of the law. If you are not, you will certainly not enter the kingdom of heaven. Murder
I heard what was said to the people who lived long ago. It was said to them, "You shall not murder. (Exodus 20:13) And whoever murders will be put to death."
Here is what I say to you. Do not be angry with your brother. Whoever is angry with his brother will be brought to judgment. Again, whoever says to his brother, "Raca!" must be brought before the Sanhedrin. But whoever says, "You fool!" will be in danger of hellfire.
Pretend you are offering your gift at the altar. And remember, your brother has something against you.
Leave your gift in front of the altar. First, go and be reconciled with your brother; then come and offer your gift.
Suppose someone has a claim against you and takes you to court. Settle the matter quickly. Do it while you are still with him on your way. If you do not do that, he may deliver you to the judge. The judge may deliver you to the officer. And you may be thrown into prison.
What I am going to tell you is true. You are not leaving until you pay the last penny! Adultery.
I heard it said, 'You shall not commit adultery.' (Exodus 20:14)
But here is what I tell you. Do not even look at a woman the wrong way. Whoever does that has already committed adultery with her in his heart.
If your right eye causes you to sin, tear it out and throw it away. Your eye is only a part of your body. It is better to lose it than to have your whole body thrown into hell.
If your right hand causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away. Your hand is merely a part of your body. It is better to lose it than to have your entire body go to hell. Divorce.
It was said, "Suppose a man puts away his wife. If he does that, he must give her a certificate of divorce." (Deuteronomy 24:1)
Here is what I say to you. Whoever puts away his wife causes her to commit adultery. And whoever marries the divorced woman commits adultery. A man cannot put away his wife unless she is not faithful to him. I swear by God.
Again, you have heard what was said to your people long ago. They were told, "Do not break the promises you have made to the Lord. Keep the vows you have made to Him."
But here is what I tell you. Do not make any such promises at all. Do not make them in heaven's name. This is God's throne.
Do not make them, the two of them, in the name of the land. That is the throne for the feet of God. Do not make them, the two of them, in the name of Jerusalem. That is the city of the great king.
And do not take an oath by your heads. You cannot make a single hair white or black.
Let "yes" mean "yes" and let "no" mean "no." All that is more than this comes from the evil one. Be kind to others.
I heard that it was said, 'An eye for an eye, and a tooth for a tooth.' (Exodus 21:24; Leviticus 24:20; Deuteronomy 19:21)
But here is what I tell you. Do not fight an evil person. "Suppose someone strikes you on your right cheek. Then present to him your left cheek also.
Suppose someone takes you to court for your shirt. Let him take your coat as well.
Assume someone forces you to walk one mile. Go two miles with him.
Give to anyone who asks something of you. Do not turn away from anyone who wants to borrow something from you. Love your enemies.
I heard them say, "Love your neighbor. (Leviticus 19:18) Hate your enemy."
But here is what I tell you. Love your enemies. Pray for those who hurt you.
"Then you will be called children of the Most High. 'He causes the sun to shine on the evil and the good, and sends rain on the righteous and the unrighteous.'
If you love those who love you, what will you gain? Even tax collectors do that.
If you only greet those who are from your tribe, what more are you doing than others? Even people who are unbelievers do that.
Be perfect, therefore, as your heavenly Father is perfect.
Giving to people in need.
Beware of doing good deeds before people. Do not do it so that people can see it. If you do that, your Father in heaven will not reward you.
When you give to people who are in need, do not announce it by blowing trumpets. Do not be like those who only pretend to be holy. They announce what they do in the temples and in the streets. They want others to honor them. What I am going to tell you is true. They have received their full reward.
When you give to the needy, do not let your left hand know what your right hand is doing.
Then you will be secretly given. Your father will reward you. He sees what you secretly do. Prayer
When you pray, don't be like the hypocrites. They love to stand and pray in the temples and on the street corners. They want to be seen by others. What I tell you is true. They have received their full reward.
When you pray, go into your room, and close the door and pray to your Father who is in secret; and your Father who sees what is done in secret
When you pray, do not babble on as the unbelievers do. They think that they will be heard because of their many words.
You are not like these people. My father knows what you need before you ask him.
This is how you should pray. "Our Father who art in heaven, hallowed be thy name. "
Your kingdom come. Your will be done on earth as it is in heaven.
Give us this day our daily bread.
Forgive us our trespasses, as we also forgive those who trespass against us.
Deliver us from evil temptation when we are vulnerable. Ransack us from Satan.
Forgive others if they wrong you. If you do this, your Father in heaven will also forgive you.
But if you do not forgive people their sins, then your father will not forgive your sins. Fasting.
When you go without eating, you do not look as sad as those who only pretend to be holy. They make their faces very sad. They want to show people that they are fasting. What I am going to tell you is true. They have received their full reward.
But when you go out without eating, put olive oil on your head. Wash your face.
Then the others will not know that you are fasting. Only your unseen Father will know. He will reward you. Your Father sees what is done in secret. Lay up treasures in heaven
Do not make for yourselves treasures on earth. The moth and rust consume them, and the thieves break in and steal them.
Instead, lay up for yourselves treasures in heaven. For where your treasure is, there your heart will be also. The lamps of the body are
Your heart will be where your treasure is.
The eye is like a candle for the body. If your eyes are good, your whole body will be filled with light.
But if your eyes are bad, your whole body will be filled with darkness. If the light within you is darkness, then it will be very dark!
No one can serve two masters at the same time. Either he will hate one and love the other, or he will be devoted to one and hate the other. You cannot serve both God and Money. Do not worry.
He told me, "Do not worry. Do not worry about your life, what you will eat or drink. And do not worry about your body, what you will wear. Is there more to life than eating? Are there not more important things for the body than clothes?"
Look at the birds of the air; for they neither sow nor reap nor gather into barns. Yet your heavenly Father feeds them. Are you not of more value than they?
Can you add one hour to your life by worrying?
And why do clothes worry you? Look how the wild flowers grow. They do not work or make clothes.
But here is what I tell you. Even Solomon in all his glory did not wear clothing like those flowers.
If God clothes the wild grass, will he not clothe you more than grass? For grass is here today and tomorrow is thrown into the fire. Your faith is too small!
So don't worry. Don't say, "What will we eat?" or, "What will we drink?" or, "What will we wear?"
People who don't care about religion are seeking all those things. My Father who is in heaven knows that you need it.
And make it clear to them that they should first present the Kingdom of God. And do what God wants you to do. And then all these things will be given to you as well.
So don't worry about tomorrow. Tomorrow will take care of itself. Every day has enough trouble of its own.
Be just when you judge others.
Do not judge others. For you will not be judged.
You will be judged by the same standard you judge others. You will be measured by the same measure you measure others.
You see a little sawdust in your friend's eye. But you don't care about the piece of wood in your own eye.
How can you tell your friend, "Let me get that little splinter out of your eye"? How can you say this when there's a piece of wood in your own eye?
"O claimant! First, remove the wooden beam from your own eye. Then you will be able to clearly see to remove the speck of sawdust from your friend's eye."
Do not give what is holy to the dogs. Do not throw your pearls before swine. If you do, they may trample them under their feet. Then they will turn and tear you to pieces. Ask, seek, knock
Ask, and it will be given to you. Seek, and you will find. Knock, and the door will be opened for you.
Everyone who asks will receive. Whoever seeks will find. The door will be opened for the one who knocks.
Suppose your son asks for bread. Which of you would give him a stone?
If he asked for a fish, which of you would give him a snake?
And if you are evil, you know how to give good gifts to your children. How much more will your Father in heaven give good gifts to those who ask him!
In everything, do to others what you would have them do to you. This is what is written in the Torah and in the Prophets. The great and small gates.
Enter the Kingdom of God, the narrow gate is at hand. The gate is great and the way is wide that leads to death and hell. Many people go this way.
But the door is small and the path is narrow, leading to life. Only a very few find it. A tree and its fruit.
Beware of false prophets, which come to you in sheep's clothing, but inwardly they are ravening wolves.
You can know what they really are by what they do. "Do people pick grapes from thorny bushes? Do they pick figs from thistles?"
For the same method, every good tree bears good fruit. But a bad tree bears bad fruit.
A good tree cannot bear bad fruit. And a bad tree cannot bear good fruit.
Every tree that does not bear good fruit is cut down. It is thrown into the fire.
You can know every tree by its fruits.
Not everyone who says to me, 'Lord, Lord,' will enter the kingdom of heaven, but only the one who does the will of my Father who is in heaven.
Many will say to me on that day, "Lord, Lord! Did we not prophesy in your name? Did we not cast out demons in your name? Did we not perform many miracles in your name?"
Then I will clearly tell them, "I never knew you. Away from me, you evildoers!" The builders, some wise and some foolish.
Therefore, everyone who hears my words and acts on them will be like a wise man who built his house on the rock.
The water rises. The wind blows and beats against that house. But it does not fall. For it is built on the rock.
But everyone who hears my words and does not apply them is like a foolish man who built his house on the sand.
The water is rising. The wind is blowing and hitting that house. And it collapses with a loud crashing sound."
Jesus finished saying all these things. The crowds were amazed at his teaching.
He taught like one who had authority. He did not teach like their teachers in the law.
Jesus heals a man who suffered from a skin disease.
Jesus came down from the mountain. Large crowds followed him.
A man who was suffering from a skin disease came and prostrated himself before Jesus. He said, "Lord, if you are willing to make me 'clean', you can do it."
Jesus reached out his hand and touched the man. "I am willing to do so," he said. "Be 'clean'!" Immediately the man was healed of his skin disease.
Then Jesus said to him, "Do not tell anyone. Go and show yourself to the priest. Offer the gift that Moses commanded. This will be a testimony to them." A Roman leader who has faith
When Jesus entered Capernaum, a Roman centurion came to him. He asked Jesus for help.
He said, "Oh Lord, your servant is sick in his house and cannot move. He is suffering greatly."
Jesus said, "I will go and heal him."
The captain replied, "Lord, I am not worthy to have you enter my home. But just say the word, and my servant will be healed.
I myself am a man under authority. And I have soldiers who obey my orders. I tell this one, "Go," and he goes. I tell that one, "Come," and he comes. I say to my servant, "Do this," and he does it."
When Jesus heard this, he was amazed. He said to those who were following him, "What I am about to tell you is true. I have not found anyone in Israel with such great faith.
He said to you, 'Behold, many are coming from the east and the west, and they will take their places at the feast in the kingdom of heaven. And they will be seated with Abraham and Isaac and Jacob in the kingdom of heaven.'
But the ones who believe they belong to the kingdom will be thrown out, into the darkness. There they will weep and grind their teeth."
Then Jesus said to the Roman general, "Go! It will be exactly as you believed it would be." And he healed his servant at that very hour. Jesus heals many people
When Jesus came to Peter's house, he saw Peter's mother-in-law. She was lying down on the bed. She was suffering from a fever.
Jesus touched her hand, and the fever left her. She arose and began to serve him.
When evening came, many people who were possessed by demons were brought to Jesus. He cast out the spirits with a word. He healed all who were sick.
He did this to fulfill what the prophet Isaiah had said. He said, "He has suffered the things that we should have suffered. He has taken the disease that we should have carried." (Isaiah 53:4) The cost of following Jesus.
Jesus saw the crowd around him. He told his disciples to go to the other side of the Sea of Galilee.
Then a teacher of the law came to him and said, "Teacher, I will follow you wherever you go."
Jesus answered, "The foxes have holes and the birds of the air have nests, but the Son of Man has nowhere to lay his head."
Another follower said to him, "Lord, let me first go and bury my father."
But Jesus said to him, "Follow Me. Let the dead bury their dead." Jesus Calms the Storm
Jesus got into the boat. His disciples followed him.
Suddenly, a terrible storm fell upon the lake. The waves swept over the boat. But Jesus was sleeping.
The students went and woke him up. They said, "Oh Lord! Save us! We are going to drown!"
He replied, "Your faith is too small! Why are you so afraid?" Then Jesus got up and ordered the winds and the waves to stop. And immediately it became perfectly calm.
The disciples were astonished. They asked, "What kind of man is this? Even the winds and the waves obey him!" Jesus Heals Two Men Possessed by Demons
Jesus crossed over to the other side of the lake, in the region of the Gerasenes. Two men who were possessed by demons met him. They had come from the tombs. The men were so dangerous that nobody could travel on that road.
"Sons of God, what do you want from us?" they cried out. "Have you come here to punish us before the time of judgment upon us?"
Not far distant, a large herd of swine was feeding.
The demons pleaded with Jesus, "If you drive us out, send us into the herd of pigs."
Jesus said to them, "Go!" So the demons came out of the men and entered the pigs. The entire herd rushed down the steep hill. They entered the lake and drowned in the water.
Those who were tending the pigs fled. They went to the city and reported all this. They told the people what had happened to the men who were under the control of the demons.
Then the entire city went out to welcome Jesus. When they saw him, they pleaded with him to leave their region.
Jesus heals a man who could not walk.
Jesus entered a boat. He went to the other side of the lake and came to his own town.
Some men brought him a man who could not walk. He was lying on a mat. Jesus saw that they had faith. He said to the man, "Do not be afraid, Son. Your sins are forgiven."
Then some of the teachers of the law said to themselves, "This fellow is saying terrible things!"
Jesus knew what they were thinking. So he said, "Why are there evil thoughts in your hearts?"
Is it easier to say, "Your sins are forgiven"? Or to say, "Rise up and walk"?
I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." Then He spoke to the man who could not walk. "Get up," He said. "Take your mat and go to your home."
The man got up and went home.
When the crowd saw this, they were filled with awe. They praised God for giving such authority to men. Jesus Chooses Matthew
And after that, as Jesus was walking from there, he saw a man called Matthew. And he was sitting at a place of a tax collector. So Jesus said to him, "Follow me." So Matthew arose and followed him.
After that, Jesus was having dinner at Matthew's house. Many tax collectors and "sinners" came and ate with Jesus and his disciples.
The Pharisees saw this. They asked the disciples, "Why does your teacher eat with tax collectors and sinners?"
Jesus heard this. And he said, "Those who are in good health do not need a physician; it is the sick who need one."
Go and learn what this means: "I desire mercy and not sacrifice." (Hosea 6:6) I did not come for those who think they are right with God to come after me. I came for sinners to come after me." Jesus is asked about fasting.
One day, the disciples of John came. They said to Jesus, "We and the Pharisees fast often. Why do your disciples not fast?"
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom mourn while he is with them? The time will come when the bridegroom will be taken from them; then they will fast."
People do not put a new piece of cloth on old clothes. The new piece will tear away from the old. This will make the hole worse.
"The people do not pour new wine into old bottles. If they do, the bottles will burst. The wine will spill out, and the bottles will be ruined. No, everyone pours the new wine into new bottles. Then both are preserved." A dead girl and a suffering woman.
While Jesus was saying this, a ruler came and knelt down before Jesus. He said, "My daughter has just died. But come and lay your hand on her, and she will live again."
Jesus rose up and went with him. And so did his disciples.
Suddenly, a woman came from behind Jesus. She had been suffering from a disease that made her bleed. This disease had lasted for 12 years. She touched the edge of his clothes.
She said to herself, "I just need to touch his clothes. Then I will be healed."
Jesus returned and saw her. "Do not despair, Madam," he said. "Your faith has made you well." The woman was healed at that moment.
When Jesus entered the ruler's house, he saw the flute players there. And he saw the noisy crowd.
He said, "Go. The girl is not dead. She is sleeping." But they laughed at him.
After the crowd was sent outside, Jesus entered. He took the girl by her hand, and she stood up.
News of what Jesus had done spread throughout the region. Jesus heals two blind men.
And while Jesus was still going on from there, two blind men followed him. And crying out, they said, "Have mercy on us, O Son of David!"
When Jesus went inside, the blind men came to him. He asked them, "Do you believe that I can do this?" "Yes, Lord," they answered.
Then he touched their eyes. He said, "It will happen to you as you have believed."
They can see again now. Jesus warned them sternly, "Make sure no one knows about this."
But they went out and spread the news. They talked about it all over the region.
And while they were coming out, another man was brought to Jesus. The devil had control over him, and he could not speak.
When the devil was cast out, the man spoke. The crowds were astonished. They said, "Nothing like this has ever been seen in Israel."
But the Pharisees said, "He casts out demons by the prince of demons." There are only a few workers.
Jesus traveled throughout all the cities and villages. He taught in their synagogues, preaching the good news of the kingdom. And he healed every disease and illness.
When he saw the crowds, he felt a deep concern for them. They were exhausted and helpless, like sheep without a shepherd.
Then Jesus said to his disciples: "The harvest is plentiful. But there are few workers.
Ask the Lord of the harvest to send out workers into his harvest field."
Jesus sends the twelve disciples.
Jesus came and called his twelve disciples to come to him. He gave them authority to cast out demons and to heal every disease and sickness.
These are the names of the twelve apostles. The first is Simon Peter, and his brother Andrew. Then comes James, the son of Zebedee, and his brother John.
The following are Philip and Bartholomew, and Thomas and Matthew the tax collector. And two others are James the son of Alphaeus and Thaddaeus.
The other two are Simon the Zealot and Judas Iscariot. Judas is the one who would later betray Jesus to his enemies.
About Jesus he sent these twelve with the following commands. "Do not go to those who are not Jews," he said. "Do not enter any city of the Samaritans.
Instead, go to the lost sheep of the house of Israel. They are like lost sheep.
As you go, preach this message, "The Kingdom of Heaven is at hand."
Heal the sick. Raise the dead. Make those with skin diseases 'clean' again. Drive out demons. You received freely, so give freely.
Do not carry with you any gold, silver, or copper in your belts.
Do not take a suitcase for the trip. Do not take extra clothes, sandals, or walking sticks. The worker should be given what he needs.
When you enter a city or a village, look for someone who is willing to welcome you. Stay at the house of this person until you leave.
Upon entering the house, say goodbye to those living there.
If that house receives you, give it your blessing of peace. If it does not, do not bless it.
Some people may not welcome you or listen to your words. If they do not, shake the dust off your feet as you leave that house or town.
What I am going to tell you is true. On the day of reckoning, it will be easier for Sodom and Gomorrah than for that city.
I am sending you out like sheep among wolves. So be wise as serpents and harmless as doves.
Danger! The men will hand you over to the local courts. They will flog you in their temples.
You will be brought before governors and kings because of me. You will be witnesses for them and for those who are not Jews.
But when they arrest you, don't worry about what you will say or how you will say it. At that time, the right words will be given to you to say.
You will not be the speaker. The spirit of your father will speak through you.
Brothers will betray each other to be killed. Fathers will betray their sons. Sons will revolt against their fathers and kill them.
Everyone will hate you because of me. But everyone who stands firm to the end will be saved.
If people are attacking you in one place, flee to another. What I tell you is true. You will not finish going through the towns of Israel before the Son of Man comes.
No student is preferred over his teacher. No servant is preferred over his master.
It suffices for a student to be like his master. And it suffices for a servant to be like his master. If the master of the house has been named Beelzebub, what can the others who live there expect?
Do not fear your enemies. What is secret will be revealed. What is hidden will be brought to light.
What I'm telling you in the dark, say it in the light. What doesn't leak into your ear, say it from the rooftops.
Do not fear those who kill the body and cannot kill the soul; rather, fear the One who is able to destroy both soul and body in hell.
Is not selling two sparrows for a farthing? But not one of them falls to the ground without your father knowing it.
He even counts every single hair on your head!
Do not be afraid. You are more valuable than many sparrows.
What about someone who says in front of others that they know me? I will also say in front of my Father who is in heaven that I know him.
But what about someone who says in front of others that they don't know me? I will say in front of my Father who is in heaven that I don't know him.
Do not suppose that I have come to bring peace to the earth. I did not come to bring peace, but a sword.
I have come to turn "sons against their fathers, and daughters against their mothers, and wives against their mothers-in-law."
The enemy who oppresses him is his own family. (Micah 7:6)
Everyone who loves his father or mother more than me is not worthy of me. Everyone who loves his son or daughter more than me is not worthy of me.
And everyone who does not pick up their cross and follow me is not worthy of me.
If anyone finds his life, he will lose it. If anyone loses his life for me, he will find it.
Whoever welcomes you welcomes me, and whoever welcomes me welcomes the one who sent me.
Suppose someone welcomes a prophet as a prophet. This will earn the reward of a prophet. And suppose someone welcomes a saint as a saint. This will earn the reward of a saint.
Assume someone gives even a cup of cold water to a little child following me. What I am going to tell you is true. This person will certainly be rewarded."
Jesus and John the Baptist.
Jesus finished teaching his twelve disciples. Then he continued to teach and preach in the cities of Galilee.
John was in prison. When he heard what Christ was doing, he sent his disciples to him.
They asked Jesus, "Are you the one who was to come? Or should we look for someone else?"
Jesus answered, "Go back to John. Tell him what you hear and what you see."
The blind see. The lame walk. Those with skin diseases are healed. The deaf hear. The dead are raised. And the Gospel is preached to the poor.
Blessed are those who do not abandon their faith because of me."
While the disciples of Jacob and John were coming out, Jesus began to speak to the crowd about Jacob. He said, "What did you go out into the desert to see? A reed shaken by the wind?"
If it were not so, what have you gone out to see? A man clothed in soft garments? No. People who are clothed in soft garments are in the palaces of kings.
So what did you go out into the wilderness to see? A prophet? Yes, I tell you, and more than a prophet.
He is the one who is mentioned in the Holy Bible. It says, "I will send my messenger to prepare the way before you." (Malachi 3:1)
What I am going to tell you is the truth. No one has ever been born who is greater than John the Baptist. But the least important person in the kingdom of heaven is greater than he is.
From the days of John the Baptist, the kingdom of heaven has been forcefully advancing. And the violent seize it by force.
All the prophets and the law prophesied until John came.
If you are willing to accept that, then John is Elijah who was supposed to come.
Let him who has ears hear.
What can I compare today's people to? They are like children sitting in the marketplaces and calling out to others. They say,
We played the flute for you. But you did not dance. We sang a funeral song. But you did not become sad.
When John came, he did not eat or drink as you do. And people say, "He has a demon."
But when the Son of Man came, he ate and drank with you, just as you do. And people say, "This man is a glutton and a drunkard, and a friend of tax collectors and sinners." But wisdom is justified by her deeds." Do not move away from sin.
Jesus began to speak against the cities where He had done most of His miracles. The people there had not left their sins behind. And He said:
"O woe to you, Chorazin! O woe to you, Bethsaida! For if the mighty works which were done in you had been done in Tyre and Sidon, they would have repented long ago in sackcloth and ashes. But it will be more tolerable for Tyre and Sidon at the judgment than for you."
But I tell you this. On the day of judgment it will be easier for the cities of Tyre and Sidon than for you.
And as for you, Capernaum, which is about to be brought down to Hades, if the miracles that were performed in you had been performed in Sodom, it would have remained until this day.
But I tell you this. On the day of reckoning, it will be easier for Sodom than for you." Comfort for all who feel tired.
At that time Jesus said, "I praise you, Father. Lord of heaven and earth, you have hidden these things from the wise and learned and revealed them to little children. Yes, Father, such is your good pleasure."
Yes, Dad. This is what you wanted.
"My father gave me everything. The father is the only one who knows the son. And the only ones who know the father are the son and those whom the son chooses to make known to him."
Strive hard and come to me, all of you who are weary and carry heavy burdens. I will give you rest.
Send forth my slaves and learn from me. I am merciful and secluded from arrogance. You will find comfort for your souls.
Serving me is easy, and my burden is light."
Jesus is the Lord of the Sabbath.
One Sabbath, Jesus walked through the grainfields. His disciples were hungry. So they began to pick some heads of grain and eat them.
The Pharisees saw this. They said to Jesus, "Look! It is against the law to do this on the Sabbath. But your disciples are doing it anyway!"
Jesus answered, "Haven't you read what David did? He and his companions were hungry.
He entered the house of God. He and his men ate the holy bread. Only the priests were allowed to eat it.
Haven't you read the law? It tells us how the priests in the temple should do their work every Sabbath day. But they are not considered guilty.
I tell you that he who is more important than the temple here.
The Holy Book said, "I desire mercy and not sacrifice." (Hosea 6:6) You do not know what these words mean. If you knew, you would not bring charges against those who are not guilty.
"The Son of man is Lord of the Sabbath."
From there, Jesus went to their synagogue.
There was a man with a withered and deformed hand. The Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. So they asked Him, "Is it lawful to heal on the Sabbath?"
He said to them, "What if one of your sheep fell into a well on the Sabbath? Wouldn't you cling to it and lift it out?"
A man is worth more than sheep! So the laws allow us to do good on the Sabbath."
Jesus said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." And he stretched it out. It was good as new, just like the other hand.
But the Pharisees went out and conspired to kill Jesus. Abdullah The Chosen One.
Jesus knew all the plans of the Pharisees. So he left that place. Many people followed him, and he healed them of every disease.
But he warned them not to tell who it was.
This would have fulfilled what was said through the Prophet Jesus. It says:
Here is my servant, whom I have chosen, whom I love, and in whom I am well pleased. I will put my Spirit upon him, and he will proclaim to the nations that the time has come for all things to be well.
He will not quarrel or shout. No one will hear his voice in the streets.
He will not break a bending branch. He will not extinguish a glowing flame that is fading. He will correct everything.
The peoples shall put their hope in him. (Isaiah 42:1-4) Jesus and Beelzebul
A man possessed by demons was brought to Jesus. The man was blind and unable to speak. Jesus healed him. Then the man was able to speak and see things.
Everyone was amazed. She said, "Can this be the son of David?"
When the Pharisees heard this, they said, "This fellow is casting out demons by the prince of demons."
Jesus knew what they were thinking. He said to them, "Every kingdom that divides against itself will be destroyed. Every city or family that is divided against itself will not stand."
If Satan casts out Satan, he is at war with himself. So how can his kingdom stand?
You say that I cast out demons by the power of Beelzebul. By what power, then, do your people cast them out? They will be your judges.
But suppose I am casting out demons by the Spirit of God. Then the kingdom of God has come upon you.
Or consider this. How can you enter a powerful man's house and take what the man owns? You must first tie him up. Then you can plunder his house.
Anyone who is not with me is against me. Anyone who does not gather the sheep with me is scattering them.
Here is what I say. Every sin and every evil word spoken against God will be forgiven. But speaking evil against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven.
Everyone who speaks a word against the Son of Man will be forgiven. But whoever speaks against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven. A person like this will not be forgiven, either now or in the days to come.
If you make a good tree, its fruits are good. And if you make a bad tree, its fruits are bad. You can know the tree by its fruits.
Your nests are of poisonous snakes! How can you evil ones say anything good? Your mouths speak all that is in your hearts.
A good man says good things. These come from the goodness that is placed within him. An evil man says evil things. These come from the evil that is placed within him.
But here is what I say to you. On the day of reckoning, people will have to give an account for every careless word they have spoken.
According to your words, you will be guilty or not guilty." Yunus's miraculous sign
Some Pharisees and teachers of the law came to Jesus. They said, "Teacher, we want to see a miraculous sign from you."
He said, "The evil and unbelieving people seek a miraculous sign! But no one will be given a sign except the sign of Jonah the prophet."
Jonah was in the belly of a great fish for three days and three nights. Something like this will happen to the Son of Man. He will spend three days and three nights in the tomb.
The men of Nineveh will stand on the day of judgment with the people who are now living. And the Ninevites will rise up to condemn those people, because they repented at what Jonah preached to them. And now there is someone greater than Jonah.
The Queen of the South will stand on the day of judgment with the people who are now living. And she will prove them guilty. She came from a very long way to listen to the wisdom of Solomon. And now, there is someone more important than Solomon.
What happens when an evil spirit comes out of a man? It goes through the dry areas, looking for a place to rest. But it doesn't find any.
Then he says, "I will go back to the house I left." And when he gets there, he finds the house empty. He had managed to clean the house and put it in good condition.
Then the unclean spirit goes out and takes with it seven other spirits more wicked than itself. They go and live there. That man is in a worse state than before. This is how it will be with the wicked people of today." Jesus' mother and brothers
While Jesus was still speaking to the crowd, his mother and brothers were standing outside. They wanted to speak to him.
Someone told him, "Your mother and brothers are standing outside. They want to speak with you."
Jesus answered him, "Who is my mother, and who are my brothers?"
Jesus said, pointing to his disciples, "Behold my mother! Behold my brethren!"
Everyone who does what my Father in heaven wants is my brother or sister or mother.
The Farmer's Story
One day Jesus left the house and sat by the Sea of Galilee.
A large crowd gathered around him. So he got into a boat. He sat in it. All the people stood on the shore.
Then he said to them many things about matters by using modern things. He said: "A farmer went for agriculture to sow his seed."
He scattered the seeds on the ground. Some fell on a path. Birds came and ate them.
Some seeds fell on rocky places, where there was not much soil. The plants grew quickly, because the soil was not deep.
When the sun came out, the plants burned. They dried up because they had no roots.
The last seeds fell among the thorns. The thorns grew and choked the plants.
Other seeds fell on good soil. They produced a harvest: 100, 60, or 30 times what was planted.
Let those who have ears hear.
The students came to him. They asked him, "Why do you use stories when you talk to people?"
He replied, "I have been given the opportunity to understand the secrets of the Kingdom of Heaven. That has not been given to foreigners."
Anyone who has this kind of knowledge will get more. In fact, they will get a lot. If no one has this kind of knowledge, even the little they have will be taken away from them.
Here is why I use stories when I talk to people. I say, "They are looking, but they do not really see. They are listening, but they do not really hear or understand.
In them, the words of the prophet Jesus are fulfilled. He said, "You will hear but not understand. You will see but not recognize what you are seeing."
The hearts of these people have become callous. They barely listen to it with their ears. They have closed their eyes. Otherwise, they might see with their eyes. They might hear with their ears. They might understand with their hearts. They might turn to the Lord, and then he would heal them. (Isaiah 6:9,10)
But blessed are your eyes because they see. And blessed are your ears because they hear.
What I am going to tell you is true. Many prophets and holy persons would have wanted to see what you see. But they did not see it. They would have wanted to hear what you hear. But they did not hear it.
Listen! Here is the meaning of the story of the farmer.
People hear the message about the kingdom, but they do not understand it. Then the evil one comes. He steals what was sown in their hearts. These people are like seed sown along the path.
Others received the seed that fell on rocky places. They are the ones who hear the message and accept it immediately with joy.
But they have no roots. So they do not last long. They soon abandon their faith when trouble or suffering comes because of the message.
Others received the seed that fell among the thorns. They are the ones who hear the message. But then, the worries of life and the promises of false riches fill them, and they choke the word, and it does not bear fruit.
Some others, however, received the seeds that fell on good soil. They are the ones who hear the message and understand it. They produce a crop 100, 60, or 30 times more than what the farmer planted." The Parable of the Weeds
Jesus told another parable to the crowds. "Thus is the kingdom of heaven," he said. "A man sowed good seed in his field.
But while everyone was sleeping, his enemy came. The enemy sowed weeds among the wheat and then left.
The wheat began to grow and form grains. At the same time, weeds appeared.
The master's servants came to him. They said, "Sir, did you not sow good seed in your field? Where did the weeds come from?"
He said, "An enemy did this," he replied. The servants asked him, "Do you want us to go and pull out the weeds?"
"No," the owner replied. "When you pull out the weeds, you might pull out the wheat along with it."
Allow both to grow together until harvest. At that time I will tell the workers what to do. Here is what I will tell them. First gather the weeds. Bind them into bundles to be burned. Then gather the wheat. Bring it to my storage."Stories of the Mustard Seed and the Leaven"
Jesus told another parable to the crowds. He said, "The Kingdom of Heaven is like a mustard seed. Someone took the seed and planted it in a field.
It is the smallest of all your seeds. But when it grows, it will be the largest plant in the whole garden. And it will become a tree. The birds will come and rest in its branches."
Jesus told them another story. "The Kingdom of Heaven is like yeast," he said. "A woman mixed it into a large amount of flour. The yeast worked its way through the entire dough."
ssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssssss
Jesus explains the parable of the weeds.
Then Jesus left the crowds and went into the house. His disciples came to him and said, "Explain to us the parable of the weed in the field."
Answer, "The one who sowed the good seed is the Son of Man."
The field is the world. The good seed represents the people who belong to the kingdom. The weeds are the people who belong to the evil one.
The enemy who sows them is the devil. The harvest is the Day of Judgment. And the workers are the angels.
"The grass is being uprooted and burned in the fire. So it will be on the day of reckoning."
The Son of Man will send his angels. They will remove all causes of sin from his kingdom. They will also remove everyone who does evil.
They will throw them into the burning, glowing charcoal. There, people will weep and grind their teeth.
Then the people of God will shine like the sun in their father's kingdom. Those who have ears must listen. Stories of hidden treasure and pearls.
The Kingdom of Heaven is like a treasure hidden in a field. When a man found it, he hid it again, and went away rejoicing greatly in himself. Therefore he went and sold all that he had, and bought that field.
Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a merchant seeking rare pearls.
He found one that was very old. So he went and sold all that he had. And he bought that pearl. The story of the net.
Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a net. That was cast into the sea. And gathered to it were all kinds of fish.
When it was full, the fishermen pulled it ashore. Then they sat down and gathered the good fish into baskets. But they threw the bad fish back into the sea.
This is how it will be on the Day of Judgment. The angels will come. They will separate the people who did what is wrong from those who did what is right.
The wicked will be cast into burning sulfur. There the wicked will weep and grind their teeth.
Jesus said, "Do you understand all these things?" And they answered, "Yes."
He said to them, "Every scribe who has been trained for the kingdom of heaven is like a master of a house who brings out from his treasure what is new and what is old." A prophet without honor.
Jesus finished telling these stories. Then he moved on from there.
He came to his hometown of Nazareth. There he began teaching the people in their synagogue. They were amazed. "Where did this man get this wisdom? Where did he get this power to do miracles?" they asked.
"Is not this the son of the carpenter? Is not his mother's name Mary? Are not his brothers James, Joseph, Simon, and Judas?"
Not all of his sisters are with us, so where did this man get all these things from?
They were not at all satisfied with him. But Jesus said to them, "A prophet is not without honor except in his own country and in his own house."
Because they were not believers, few miracles were performed there.
John the Baptist's head is being cut off.
At that time, Herod Antipas, the ruler of Galilee and Perea, heard reports about Jesus.
He told his servants, "This is John the Baptist. He has risen from the dead! That is why he has the power to perform miracles."
Herod had arrested John, bound him, and put him in prison because of Herodias. She was the wife of Herod's brother Philip.
John was saying to Herod, "It is against the law for you to have her."
Herod wanted to kill John, but he was afraid of the people, because they considered John to be a prophet.
On Herod's birthday, Herodias's daughter danced for Herod and his guests. She pleased Herod greatly.
He promised her on oath that he would give her anything she asked for.
Her mother said to her, "What is it you want?" And the girl said to Herod, "I want the head of John the Baptist on a big plate."
The king was very angry. But he thought of his promise and his guests. So he said to one of his men to give her what she asked for.
Herod had John beheaded in prison.
His head was brought on a large platter and given to the girl. Then she carried it to her mother.
John's disciples came and took his body and buried him. Then they went and told Jesus. Jesus feeds the five thousand.
Jesus heard what had happened to John. He wanted to be alone. So he went in a boat to a quiet place. The crowds heard about this. They followed him on foot from the towns.
When Jesus arrived at the shore, he saw a large crowd. He felt deep concern for them. He healed their sick.
When evening came, the disciples went to him and said, "This is a deserted place, and it is already late. Send the crowds away, so they can go and buy food in the villages."
Jesus replied, "They don't need to go away. Give them something to eat."
They said to him, "We have only five loaves of bread and two fish."
He said, "Bring them here to me."
Then Jesus had the people sit down on the grass. He took the five loaves and the two fish, looked up to heaven and thanked God. He broke the loaves into pieces, then gave them to the disciples, who passed them on to the people.
They all ate and were satisfied. The disciples gathered twelve baskets of leftover pieces.
About 5000 men ate. Women and children also ate. Jesus walks on water.
Immediately Jesus made the disciples get into the boat. And he commanded them to go to the other side of the Sea of Galilee. Then he sent the crowd away.
After sending them away, he went up into a solitary mountain to pray. When evening came, he was there alone.
The boat was far from the shore. The waves were hitting it because the winds were blowing on it.
Early in the morning, Jesus went out to the disciples. He walked on the lake.
They saw him walking on the lake, so they were afraid. "He is a ghost!" they said. And they cried out of fear.
Immediately Jesus called out to them, "Be courageous! I am here. Do not be afraid."
"Lord, are you?" Peter asked. "If it is you, tell me to come to you on the water."
Jesus said, "Come." So Peter got out of the boat. He walked on the water toward Jesus.
But when Peter saw the wind, he was afraid. He began to sink. He cried out, "Lord! Save me!"
Immediately Jesus stretched out His hand and caught him. "Your faith is too small!" He said. "Why did you doubt Me?"
When they climbed into the boat, the wind stopped.
Then those who were in the boat worshiped Jesus. They said, "You are truly the Son of God!"
They crossed the lake and disembarked at Gennesaret.
The men who lived there acknowledged Jesus. Therefore, they sent a message throughout the nearby countryside. The people brought all their sick to Jesus.
When they asked him to allow the sick to touch the edge of his cloak, they begged him to do so. And everyone who touched him was healed.
What makes people "unclean"?
Some Pharisees and some teachers of the law who had come from Jerusalem approached Jesus and asked him,
Why do your disciples not live according to the teachings of the elders? Your disciples don't wash their hands before eating!
Jesus replied, "Why do you not obey the command of God? You prefer to follow your own teachings!"
God said, "Honor your father and your mother." (Exodus 20:12; Deuteronomy 5:16) He also said, "If anyone curses his father or his mother, he will be put to death." (Exodus 21:17; Leviticus 20:9)
But you allow people to tell their parents, "Any help you have received from us is a gift intended for God."
They do not need to honor their parents with their gift. You make the word of God useless in order to follow your own teachings.
You are hidden ones! Isaiah was right when he prophesied about you. He said,
"This man angers me with what he says. While their hearts are far from me."
Their worship means nothing to me. They teach only man-made rules." (Isaiah 29:13)
Jesus came to the crowd and said, "Listen and understand."
What enters your mouth does not make it 'unclean'. It is what comes out of your mouth that makes it 'unclean'."
Then the disciples came to him. They asked him, "Do you know that the Pharisees were angered when they heard this?"
Jesus replied, "There are plants my Father in heaven has not planted; these will be pulled up by the roots."
Leave them alone. The Pharisees are blind guides. If a blind man leads a blind man, both will fall into a pit."
Peter said, "Explain this to us."
"Haven't you understood still?" Jesus asked them.
Don't you see? All that goes into the mouth, goes into the stomach. Then it comes out of the body.
But the things that come out of the mouth come from the heart. Those are the things that make you "unclean."
Evil thoughts come from the heart. So also murder, adultery, and sexual immorality. Also theft, false witness, and lying.
These are the things that make you "unclean." But eating without washing your hands does not make you "unclean." "The Faith of a Canaanite Woman"
Jesus left Galilee and went to the region of Tyre and Sidon.
A Canaanite woman who lived near Tyre and Sidon came to him and cried out, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on me!" A demon had seized her daughter, and she was suffering greatly.
Jesus didn't say a word. His disciples came to him. They pleaded, "Send her away. She's still screaming after us."
Jesus answered, "I was sent only to the lost sheep of Israel. They are like a sheep that has wandered off from the flock."
Then the woman fell to her knees before him. "Oh Lord! Help me!" she said.
Re: "It is not right that you take the children's bread and throw it to the dogs."
She said, "Yes, Lord." "But the dogs eat the crumbs that fall from their master's table."
Then Jesus answered: "Woman, great is your faith! Let it be done for you as you desire." And her daughter was instantly healed. Jesus feeds the four thousand
Jesus went out from there. He walked along the Sea of Galilee. Then he went up on the side of a mountain and sat down.
Large crowds came to him. They brought with them a blind man and those who could not walk. They also brought with them the lame, those who could not speak, and many others. They placed them at his feet, and he healed them.
The people were amazed. Those who could not speak were speaking. The disabled were healed. Those who could not walk were walking. Those who were blind could see. Therefore, the people praised the God of Israel.
Then Jesus called his disciples to come to him. He said, "I am deeply concerned about these people. They have already been with me for three days, and they have nothing to eat. I do not want to send them away hungry. If I do, they will become too weak on their journey home."
His disciples answered him. "There is nothing here," they said. "Where can we get enough bread to feed this large crowd?"
"How many loaves have you?" Jesus asked. "Seven" they answered, "and a few small fish."
Jesus said to the crowds, "Sit on the ground."
The seven loaves and the fish He took, and gave thanks. Then He broke them and gave them to the disciples. And the disciples gave them to the people.
They all ate and were satisfied. Afterward, the disciples took seven baskets of leftover pieces.
The number of men who ate food was 4000. Women and children also ate food.
After Jesus sent the crowd away, he went up into the boat. He went to the region near Magadan.
People ask Jesus for a miraculous sign.
The Pharisees and Sadducees came to test Jesus. They asked him to show them a miraculous sign from heaven.
Re: "In the evening you look at the sky. You say, "It will be beautiful. The sky is red."
And in the morning she says, "Today there will be a storm. The sky is red and overcast." You know what you see in the sky means. But you cannot understand the signs of what is happening now.
A wicked and adulterous generation seeks for a sign, but no sign will be given to it but the sign of Jonah." And Jesus left them and went away. The leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
The pupils went to the other side of the lake. They had forgotten to take bread.
Jesus said to them, "Be on your guard and beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
The pupils discussed this matter among themselves. They said, "He must be saying this because we did not bring any bread."
Jesus knew what they were saying. He said, "You have little faith! Why are you talking to each other about having no bread?"
Why do you not understand yet? Did you not remember the five loaves for the two thousand people? Did you not remember the number of baskets of fragments that you collected?
Do you not remember the seven loaves for the four thousand? Do you not remember how many baskets of fragments you gathered?
How did I misunderstand? I wasn't going to argue with you about bread. But beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
Then the disciples understood that Jesus was not telling them to be careful about the yeast used in bread. He was warning them about the teachings of the Pharisees and Sadducees. Peter says that Jesus is the Christ.
Jesus came into the region of Caesarea Philippi. There he asked his disciples, "Who do people say that the Son of Man is?"
They replied, "Some say John the Baptist; others, Elijah; still others, Jeremiah, or one of the prophets."
"Why you?" he said. "Who do you think I am?"
Peter answered and said, "You are the Christ. You are the Son of the Living God."
Jesus answered, "Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah! For this was not revealed to you by flesh and blood, but by My Father who is in heaven.
Thus I tell you. You are Peter. On this rock I will build my church. The gates of hell shall not prevail against it.
I will give you the keys to the kingdom of heaven. What you bind on earth will be bound in heaven. What you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven."
Then Jesus warned his disciples not to tell anyone that he was the Messiah. Jesus speaks about his coming death.
From that time on, Jesus began to explain to his disciples what would happen to him. He told them that he must go to Jerusalem. There he must suffer at the hands of the elders, the chief priests, and the teachers of the law. He must be killed and rise to life again on the third day.
Peter took Jesus aside and began to rebuke him. "God forbid, Lord!" he said. "This will never happen to you!"
Jesus turned and said to Peter, "Get behind me, Satan! You are a stumbling block to me. For you are not setting your mind on the things of God, but on the things of men."
Then Jesus spoke to his disciples. He said, "If anyone wants to follow me, they must say no to themselves. They must carry their cross and follow me."
If he wants to save his life, he will lose it. But if he loses his life for me, he will find it.
What good would it do to gain the whole world if he lost his own soul? Or what could a person give in exchange for his soul?
The human son will descend in the glory of his father. His angels will come with him. And he will reward each person according to what they have done.
What I am telling you is true. Some of those who are standing here will not die before they see the Son of Man coming in his kingdom.
Jesus' body is changing.
After six days, Jesus took Peter, James, and John the brother of James with him. He led them up to a high mountain. They were all by themselves.
Before them his appearance was transformed. His face shone like the sun. His clothes became white as light.
Suddenly, Moses and Elijah appeared before them. Moses and Elijah were talking with Jesus.
Peter said to Jesus, "Lord, it is good for us to be here. If you wish, I will put up three tents. One for you, one for Moses, and one for Elijah."
And while Peter was speaking, a bright cloud came about them. And a voice out of the cloud said, "This is My beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased. I Myself will give you assurance of this. Hear Him!"
And when the disciples heard this, they were afraid. They fell on their faces.
But Jesus came and touched them. "Rise," he said. "Do not be afraid."
When they looked up, they saw no one except Jesus.
They came down from the mountain. While coming down, Jesus told them what to do. "Do not tell anyone what you have seen," he said. "Wait until the Son of Man has risen from the dead."
The students asked him, "Why do the teachers of the law say that Elijah must come first?"
Jesus replied, "It is true. Elijah is supposed to come and make all things new again."
And I tell you that Elijah has come, and they did not recognize him, but did to him whatever they pleased. And so also will they do to the Son of Man."
Then the disciples understood that Jesus was speaking to them about John the Baptist. Jesus heals a boy who had a demon.
As they came near to the crowd, a man approached Jesus. He knelt down before him.
He said, "Lord, have mercy on my son. He is shaking violently and suffering greatly. He falls often into the fire or into the water.
I brought him to your disciples. But they could not heal him."
"You are the wicked and godless people!" Jesus answered. "How long must I stay with you? How long must I put up with you? Bring the boy to me."
Jesus ordered the demon to come out of the boy, and it came out. He was healed at that moment.
Then the disciples came to Jesus privately. They asked him, "Why could we not drive out the demon?"
He said, "Because your faith is very small. What I am about to tell you is true. If your faith is as small as a mustard seed, it is enough. You can say to this mountain, 'Move from here to there.' And it will move. Nothing will be impossible for you."
<.>
They met in Galilee. Then Jesus said to them, "The Son of Man will be delivered into the hands of men."
They will kill him. On the third day he will rise from the dead." Then the disciples were filled with great sorrow. Jesus pays the temple tax
When Jesus and His disciples arrived in Capernaum, the tax collectors came to Peter and asked him, "Does your teacher pay the temple tax?"
He said, "Yes, he does," he answered. When Peter entered the house, Jesus spoke first. "What do you think, Simon?" he asked. "Who among men on earth, who collect taxes and tribute from the kings of the earth, do you think they collect from their own sons or from others?"
"From others," Peter replied. "So that the children will not have to pay," Jesus told him.
But we do not want to anger them. So go to the lake and cast your fishing line. Take the first fish that you catch. Open its mouth. There you will find the exact coin that you need. Take it and give it to them for my tax and my duty.
